  "title": "Subtractive Synthesis",
  "summary": "Rich oscillator → filter → amp envelope architecture for classic analog sounds",
  "description": "Subtractive synthesis is the foundational synthesis method for most analog-style sounds. Starting with harmonically rich waveforms (sawtooth, square, triangle, pulse), the filter sculpts away frequencies to shape the tone. Key components: oscillators generate raw waveforms, filter (low-pass, high-pass, band-pass) removes frequencies, ADSR envelope shapes amplitude over time, LFO adds modulation. Famous examples: Moog Minimoog, Roland Juno-106, Arturia Mini V. Best for: classic analog-style leads, pads, and basses with warm, familiar tones. The 'subtractive' name comes from removing (subtracting) frequencies rather than adding them.",
  "source": {
    "type": "expert-tutorial",
    "reference": "research/best-practices.md#synthesis-type-selection",
    "url": "https://blog.landr.com/types-of-synthesis"
  },
  "concepts": [
    {
      "name": "Oscillator",
      "description": "Sound source generating raw waveforms (saw, square, triangle, sine)"
    },
    {
      "name": "Filter",
      "description": "Removes frequencies to shape tone (low-pass is most common)"
    },
    {
      "name": "ADSR Envelope",
      "description": "Attack-Decay-Sustain-Release shapes amplitude over time"
    },
    {
      "name": "LFO",
      "description": "Low-Frequency Oscillator for modulation (filter cutoff, pitch, amplitude)"
    }
  ],
